Based on my analysis of the site, here is a reverse engineering summary of AmaderPay – a payment gateway service for bKash, Nagad, and Rocket in Bangladesh.

🔍 Overview

AmaderPay provides a SMS-based auto-payment gateway. It uses an Android app to read SMS notifications from mobile financial services (bKash/Nagad/Rocket) and automatically verifies transactions via API.

🧱 Technology Stack

Component Technology

Frontend SvelteKit (static site generation)

Hosting Vercel (amaderpay.vercel.app)

Routing Client-side routing with SvelteKit

Styling Tailwind CSS (inferred from class names)

Icons Lucide icons

Mobile App Native Android APK (SMS reader)

🗺️ Site Structure & Routes

Route Purpose

/ Landing page with feature highlights

/docs API documentation & live testing playground

/merchant Merchant login / registration portal

/login Redirects to /merchant

(others) No /payment, /register, etc. found

🔌 API Endpoints (from /docs)

Method Endpoint Auth Description

POST /api/v1/checkout/create Bearer Token Creates a payment session, returns checkout_url

GET /api/v1/checkout/verify/:id Public Checks payment status & returns TrxID

POST /api/v1/webhook/trigger Internal Sends webhook notification to merchant on payment confirmation

The actual backend API is likely hosted separately (not on Vercel), as these are under /api/v1/ and may be proxied or served via serverless functions.

📱 Mobile App (SMS Gateway)

APK Download: https://github.com/apon133/safeCalc/releases/download/v1.2.0/sms.apk

Function: Reads SMS from the merchant’s phone, extracts TrxID, and syncs with the server.

Pairing: Uses a Device Key (format: pg_dev_...) to isolate SMS data to the merchant’s account.

Auto-verification: Claims to verify payments within 20 seconds.

🧑‍💻 Merchant Workflow

Sign up at /merchant with bKash/Nagad number.

Install the Android SMS app and pair with the Device Key.

Generate payment links from the merchant portal.

Share links with customers via Facebook/WhatsApp.

Receive webhook notifications on payment confirmation.

🧩 Observations & Notes

Frontend is static: All pages are pre-rendered SvelteKit outputs – no server-side rendering detected.

No exposed secrets: No API keys or environment variables found in the client-side code.

Limited client-side logic: Most business logic is handled server-side or via the Android app.

Potential security considerations: The system relies on SMS reading, which could be vulnerable to spoofing if not properly validated. The Device Key mechanism aims to mitigate this.

📌 Summary

AmaderPay is a SvelteKit frontend acting as a dashboard and documentation portal for a SMS-based payment verification service. The core logic lives in the Android app and a separate backend API. Merchants integrate via REST APIs and receive real-time webhook callbacks.

If you need deeper analysis (e.g., API request/response structures, Android app decompilation, or backend fingerprinting), let me know and I can dig further.










